\begin_layout Section
Intention
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Technology rewards the curious.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
I am running this workshop to equip my community with tools to better protect its safety.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
That said,
 it can be very easy to become overwhelmed and fatalistic,
 obsess over worst case scenarios,
 or become fixated on mythical 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

most secure
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

correct
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 tools and processes.
 (The only way to be perfectly secure is to talk to no one,
 do nothing of consequence,
 and never take a single risk).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We learn best when we are,
 curious,
 playful,
 and consistently engaged.
 While reading a list of expert recommendations once probably makes you a little safer than nothing,
 you are far better served improving your safety skills a little bit every week.
 Trying new tools,
 researching,
 and discussing it with people,
 especially people you don't necessarily agree with.
 If you find learning about computers or online privacy miserable,
 you aren't going to do any of that.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
I will personally consider the workshop a success if everyone who attends learns:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Something they think is cool.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Something that makes them curious to know more.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Something that equips them to be one modicum safer than they were before.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
What Is Tails?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Tails (The Amnesic & Incognito Live System) is a tool for mitigating digital surveillance and censorship.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
It is a bootable Live USB.
 You plug it into a computer,
 and boot into a temporary separate Operating System.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
By default Tails will:
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Retain no data between sessions to avoid leaving traces of what you use it for.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Route all internet traffic over the TOR network to provide privacy and anonymity while using the internet.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Terminology
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Some very quick definitions.
 A shared understanding of these terms makes us more effective when talking about digital privacy.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Anonymity - Who is talking to who?
 Metadata.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Privacy - What are they talking about?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Security - How resistant is their communication medium to interventions that downgrade it in potentially several ways
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "key-1"
literal "true"

\end_inset

:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Anonymity,
 unmask the participants.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Privacy,
 gain access to the contents of their communication.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Availability,
 take the medium down and make it unavailable for use.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Otherwise co-opt or use the medium for unintended purposes.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Graphics
	filename images/CritterAndCable.png
	scale 80

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Separator plain
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Encrypted in Transit
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Most websites use a technology called SSL,
 using the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

HTTPS
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 prefix.
 This means the traffic is encrypted between the client and the server,
 and can't be read by the ISP or anyone else listening on the network.
 It can still be read by the server.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
For example,
 Discord is a messaging app that is encrypted in transit.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Graphics
	filename images/SSLDiagram.png
	scale 70
	BoundingBox 80bp 0bp 2237bp 630bp
	clip

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
End-To-End Encrypted (E2EE)
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Services that are end-to-end encrypted mean the data is encrypted in transit,
 and also encrypted on the server.
 This is usually used to refer to messaging apps where only the intended recipient can decrypt the message.
 Typically a server is involved,
 routing the encrypted data from one client to another.
 End-to-end encrypted servcies offer privacy for the contents of messages,
 but dont usually offer anonymity,
 as metadata like who is talking to who,
 and when,
 is still visible to the server.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Signal is end-to-end encrypted,
 but not anonymous,
 and requires users to provide a phone number to use the service.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Graphics
	filename images/EndToEndDiagram.png
	scale 70

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Peer-To-Peer (P2P)
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Services that are peer-to-peer don't involve a central server at all.
 Instead individual clients connect to directly to one another.
 Usually this means both parties must be online at the same time,
 and have a way of pairing their devices either in person at a previous point,
 or via a different communication method.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Briar is a peer-to-peer messenger app.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
SimpleX and Delta Chat are mostly peer-to-peer,
 relying on servers to connect peers but take steps to run multiple servers by different parties and minimize the amount of metadata any individual server has.
 
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Graphics
	filename images/PeerToPeerDiagram.png
	scale 70

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Difficulty Differential
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Mitigation's that are easy for me,
 but hard for an attacker are the most valuable.
 Some mitigation's will be more like 50/50,
 these are less useful but not useless depending on your needs.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Risk
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Standard
Risk = Cost of bad thing * Likelihood of bad thing
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Examples:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Risk of getting in a car crash:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Cost:
 Pretty high,
 ranges from injury or death depending on the kind of accident.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
x 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Likelyhood:
 Depends on your age,
 lifestyle,
 and other factors.
 In the United States it ranked as the 13th leading cause of death among all causes in 2016 and 2017.
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "1"
literal "false"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
= Risk depends on individual factors.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
TOR
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
TOR stands for 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

The Onion Network
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 and is a privacy preserving network.
 The TOR network is typically accessed with the TOR browser.
 TOR routes all traffic through at least 3 encrypted relay nodes before being routed to its final destination.
 Traffic is encrypted in layers,
 with each relay node removing a single layer of encryption.
 This is designed to prevent any one node from knowing the source and destination of your web traffic.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Onion Services
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Onion services are websites that can only be reached via the TOR network.
 Some websites are only accessible via Onion services (the so-called 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Dark web
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

),
 while other websites have clearnet sites,
 while also offering Onion sites as a privacy preserving option for users.
 
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Section
Why Should I Use Tails?
 Things Tails Can and Cannot do.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
What Tails Does well:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Keeps web traffic anonymous and private from internet service providers or other people on the network.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Keeps you anonymous from the website you are visiting,
 provided you don't supply identifying information.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Provides decent security against most threats.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
What Tails Doesn't do well:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Hide from your Internet Service provider you are using TOR.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Hide from the website you are visiting that you are using TOR.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Protect your anonymity if you do something that unmasks you.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Provide security against threat actors with nation-state level resources.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Threat Model
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Whether Tails is an appropriate tool for you depends on what you are trying to do and your threat model.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
A Threat Model is a structured way of thinking about your goals and obstacles or people who may not want to you accomplish those goals.
 Several formalized frameworks exist.
 I'll be using the framework outlined in the Zine 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Threat Modeling Fundamentals
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 by Håkan Geijer.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "key-2"
literal "true"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
What are our goals?
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Who or what might oppose or complicate those goals?
 What kinds of tools do they have?
 What risk do they pose?
 What would the impact be?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
What can we do about it?
 Do we have a mitigation that brings risk down to negligible levels?
 What about mitigations that reduces risk a little,
 but still has a tangible likelihood?
 Or is this a risk we are forced to accept?
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Is our threat model well informed?
 Is it effective?
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\end_deeper
\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Graphics
	filename images/ThreatModelDiagram.png
	scale 70

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Below are additional resources on threat modeling
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Frameworks:
 
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
HARMS framework focuses less on technical treats and more on human impact:
 https://arxiv.org/html/2502.07116v1
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
This zine explores threat modeling in more detail and is tailored towards activists:
 https://opsec.riotmedicine.net/downloads#threat-modeling-fundamentals
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
One potential place to research threats and mitigations:
 https://www.notrace.how/threat-library/
\end_layout

\end_deeper
